he same per move (2.6 for Opus 5.5, 2.7 for the 10k), but Opus 5.5's biggest slips came at the worst moments.", "moments": [{"turn": 21, "text": "Opus 5.5's E8 lost 9.2 points and dropped Black's lead from -11.0 to -20.2, the deepest hole of the game. The 10k's F8 reply lost 8.5 itself, so the position came back to -11.7 and the game stayed open."}, {"turn": 26, "text": "The 10k's E1 lost 24.3 points and flipped Black's lead from -8.2 to +16.1. The engine preferred D2 here. For one move Black was comfortably ahead."}, {"turn": 27, "text": "Black's F1 lost 28.0 points, the worst move of the game, and the lead fell from +16.1 straight back to -11.9. The engine's choice was B4, which Opus 5.5 only played two turns later. The gift from move 26 lasted exactly one turn."}, {"turn": 49, "text": "With the game close at -2.2, Opus 5.5 played G7 into White's upper right and lost 10.5 points, taking the lead to -12.7. The 10k's G8 answer lost 10.6 itself and pulled it back to -2.1. The engine's choice was G2."}, {"turn": 51, "text": "Opus 5.5 passed with the game at -2.1 and the engine still wanted G2. The pass lost 10.5 points and put the lead at -12.7. White then took H2, G2 and H1 along the bottom, and the game ended W+10."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py only parses a pasted board and prints each group's stones and liberties, and the other commands just read or append to games.md notes; nothing chooses, ranks, or looks ahead at moves.", "stop": false}}